WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:07.920
The first film showed how antibodies and mass spectrometry find one phosphate among

2
00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:09.120
thousands of proteins.

3
00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:12.840
Finding that mark is only half the problem.

4
00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:18.600
Roughly 500 human kinases share a similar ATP pocket.

5
00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:23.020
How do you prove which kinase added it, and caused the response?

6
00:00:24.183 --> 00:00:27.703
Two obvious tests both fail to find direct targets.

7
00:00:28.903 --> 00:00:34.783
Add ordinary radioactive ATP to an extract, and every active kinase labels its

8
00:00:34.783 --> 00:00:35.883
substrates at once.

9
00:00:37.083 --> 00:00:39.003
Or remove kinase X from cells.

10
00:00:40.383 --> 00:00:44.863
Phosphorylation drops on direct targets and on proteins three steps downstream,

11
00:00:45.423 --> 00:00:46.423
looking identical.

12
00:00:47.317 --> 00:00:52.277
Kevan Shokat's laboratory solved this by reshaping one kinase's pocket.

13
00:00:53.597 --> 00:00:58.577
In a normal kinase, a bulky gatekeeper residue narrows the ATP pocket.

14
00:00:59.937 --> 00:01:04.997
Mutate that gatekeeper in kinase X to small glycine or alanine, opening a hole.

15
00:01:06.357 --> 00:01:11.797
Now supply a bulky benzyl ATP analog carrying a radioactive gamma phosphate.

16
00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:18.590
The bulky benzyl group fits normal pockets poorly, so other kinases keep using

17
00:01:18.590 --> 00:01:20.230
ordinary ATP silently.

18
00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:27.910
Only engineered, analog-sensitive kinase X accepts the bulky analog and transfers

19
00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:30.930
its radioactive phosphate to direct substrates.

20
00:01:31.990 --> 00:01:36.050
The bulky group stays behind on adenine; only the phosphate moves.

21
00:01:37.133 --> 00:01:41.413
Next, test whether kinase X actually causes the cell's response.

22
00:01:43.173 --> 00:01:47.033
Labs often add a chemical inhibitor that plugs the ATP pocket.

23
00:01:48.433 --> 00:01:53.233
Because hundreds of kinases share that pocket, selectivity is hard to achieve.

24
00:01:54.713 --> 00:02:00.813
Large panel surveys found that many widely used inhibitors hit several kinases, not

25
00:02:00.813 --> 00:02:02.353
just the one they are sold for.

26
00:02:03.367 --> 00:02:06.227
That makes an unselective inhibitor one-sided.

27
00:02:07.587 --> 00:02:13.147
If X and many other kinases are shut off, yet the response survives, X is ruled out.

28
00:02:14.347 --> 00:02:18.227
If the response stops, X or any off-target could be responsible.

29
00:02:19.687 --> 00:02:25.467
Express a drug-resistant mutant of X. If the response returns while off-targets stay

30
00:02:25.467 --> 00:02:27.787
blocked, the drug acted through X.

31
00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:33.230
Two words organise every causal test: necessary and sufficient.

32
00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:37.730
A step is necessary if removing it stops the response.

33
00:02:39.070 --> 00:02:43.510
It is sufficient if turning it on alone triggers the response without hormone.

34
00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:49.730
Suppose an always-active kinase X triggers the full response without hormone.

35
00:02:50.990 --> 00:02:53.830
Does that prove X is necessary when hormone arrives?

36
00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:05.110
No, because when hormone arrives, a parallel pathway could still drive the response

37
00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:10.630
without X. Turning X on alone shows it is sufficient, not necessary.

38
00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:17.410
Every method fails in its own way: inhibitors hit off-targets, and knockouts disrupt

39
00:03:17.410 --> 00:03:23.050
whole networks. Causal claims earn belief when two methods that fail differently

40
00:03:23.050 --> 00:03:23.470
agree.
